  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday) Issued at 142 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned shortwave exits eastern NM early in the day\non Friday, another short-wave moves into SW NM toward afternoon,\ncutting across the state through Friday night. This shortwave will\nhave the support of widespread lift from the left-exit region of \nan H25 subtropical jet moving into northern Mexico, especially\nover southern portions of the state. This is consistent with the\nWPC Slight Risk ERO area nosing into the Gila Wilderness, within a\nlarger Marginal area. QPF amounts with this short-wave are not\noverly impressive, but localized flash flooding could be a \nconcern if enough instability is present.\n\nSaturday (Halloween), at least during the daytime, should see a\nrelative break between systems, as flat/short-wave ridging sets \nup between the departing aforementioned system and the next \ndigging southern stream trough off of California. Evening \ntemperatures in the 50s-60s F and generally light winds should be \nfavorable for outdoor activities.\n\nAlthough confidence is generally increasing regarding\nprecipitation from the next significant storm system crossing New\nMexico from Sunday through early week, some details still remain \nto be sorted out. Differences are still apparent in the ensemble \nclustering regarding the degree of phasing between northern and \nsouthern stream components of the system, though a good Pacific \nmoisture tap should be in play regardless. NBM highest PoPs and \nQPF favor the window from late Saturday night through late Sunday \nnight, with upslope areas of the Continental Divide and central \nmountain chain most favored, with 1-2 inches of liquid-equivalent \nprecip. WPC has 0.5-1.0 inches falling in the Farmington area \nduring this window, but close to a gradient with higher amounts \nfarther north. QPF in the San Juan mountains of Colorado, \ncurrently in the 1-2 inch range, will need to be closely monitored\nfor any potential hydrological issues downstream on the Animas \nand San Juan rivers by Monday. Snow levels still progged to hover \naround 10,000 ft. MSL (as H7 temps stay around 0C in the GFS, for \nexample), but confidence isn't especially high, as differences in \nthe phasing and ultimate digging of the northern-stream \ntrough/low will determine this.\n\nBy Tuesday, deterministic models and ensemble systems end up in a\npretty similar place, with a seasonally deep low in the \ncentral/southern Plains with windy and brisk conditions on its \nbackside, especially for NE NM. A wrap-around vort max could bring\nsome light rain on the plains and additional light snow in the \nnorthern mountains, and POTNL the highland zones as well.",
